.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-UPDATE-ROLE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-update-role \- replace what a role grants
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam update-role
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-permission | \-p
.I PERMISSION
.RI [ PERMISSION ...]
[\fB\-\-description\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Replaces the permissions of one of your account's roles.
.SH IT REPLACES, IT DOES NOT MERGE
The role holds exactly the permissions given here afterwards. One left
out is taken away.
.PP
That is the whole reason it works this way: there is no other way to
narrow a role. A merging update could only ever add, and a role that can
only grow is not something anybody can safely correct. So the call states
the role's permissions in full, and the risk is the visible one \- that a
list is written out incompletely \- rather than the invisible one, that a
permission nobody meant to keep stays forever.
.PP
Run
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1)
first and edit what it prints.
.SH WHEN IT TAKES EFFECT
At once, for everyone. Every grant of the role \- every principal, in
every namespace, in every account that binds it \- is affected by the
same call, because a grant names the role rather than copying it. That is
what makes a role worth having and what makes narrowing one worth
checking first:
.B euclid-cli eam list-grants --role <name>
shows who is about to be affected.
.PP
The change is not retroactive to requests already answered, and there is
no cache to wait out: a request authorized after this call uses the new
permissions.
.SH WHAT CANNOT BE CHANGED
The seven built-in roles. They are computed from the permission vocabulary
rather than stored \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1)
\- so there is nothing to write to. To have
.I operator
with one permission removed, create a role of your own that says so.
.PP
The role's name is the key and so cannot be changed either. Create the
new name, move the grants, delete the old one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Role name. Must be one of your account's own roles. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-permission " " \fIPERMISSION\fR
The role's permissions after this call \-
.IR <module>:<action> ,
.I <module>:*
or
.IR *:* .
Repeat the option, or give several after one. Required, at least once: a
role with no permissions grants nothing, which is
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1)'s
job rather than this one's.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the role is for. Replaced like everything else, so leaving it out
clears it.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The role was updated; its JSON representation is printed.
.TP
.B 1
No name or no permission given, a permission no module answers, no role
of that name in your account, a built-in name, or the caller is not an
administrator. If the failure was an expired or invalid session
(\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# See what it holds now
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam get-role \e
    --name order-service

# Who is about to be affected
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam list-grants \e
    --role order-service

# Take away the ability to delete, keeping the rest
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam update-role \e
    --name order-service \e
    --permission ens:publish-message \e
    --permission eqs:receive-messages \e
    --description "The order service's own rights"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
